{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1319,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s lots of physics going on in raindrops:\ncohesion, adhesion, air resistance – I"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5859,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mean, falling raindrops often look more like\njellyfish than teardrops – but perhaps most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10269,
    "dDurationMs": 3461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fascinating is the physics that makes raindrops\nimpossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13730,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might think making a raindrop is easy\n– just cool water vapor in the air past"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17410,
    "dDurationMs": 3170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its condensation point, and it condenses into\nliquid droplets, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20580,
    "dDurationMs": 4939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s a big problem standing, almost\nliterally, in the way: the surface of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25519,
    "dDurationMs": 1391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "droplets themselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26910,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Liquids hate surfaces – they're bound by\nthe laws of intermolecular attraction to pull"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31400,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together in an attempt to minimize the size\nof their surfaces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34260,
    "dDurationMs": 3991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s why small water droplets are spherical,\nwhy you can put a huge amount of water on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38251,
    "dDurationMs": 3669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a penny, and why bubbles form the crazy shapes\nthey do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41920,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The technical way of saying this is that surfaces\nrequire more free energy to make than volumes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47180,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, when you’re condensing water\nin saturated air from a gas to a liquid, every"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51320,
    "dDurationMs": 4579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cubic centimeter VOLUME of water you make\nreleases energy just from its change of volume"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55899,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and pressure – roughly enough to lift an\napple a meter into the air."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56899,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to make each square centimeter of the\nSURFACE of that water requires an INPUT of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60879,
    "dDurationMs": 4731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy – not much, but it's equivalent to\nlifting a fortune cookie fortune 1 centimeter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65610,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For large amounts of water, the energy you\nget from the volume, which is proportional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68970,
    "dDurationMs": 3881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the radius cubed, is more than enough to\nmake up for the energy cost due to the surface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72851,
    "dDurationMs": 2959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "area, which is proportional to the radius\nsquared."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75810,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cubing tends to make things bigger than squaring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78310,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "BUT for really small radii, the opposite is\ntrue – cubing a small number makes it smaller"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82920,
    "dDurationMs": 1370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than squaring it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84290,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This unavoidable mathematical truth means\nthat if a water droplet is below a certain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88060,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size, then making it bigger requires more\nsurface area energy than is released from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92540,
    "dDurationMs": 5109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "volume energy, meaning it TAKES energy for\nthe droplet to grow, so it doesn’t – it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97649,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shrinks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98649,
    "dDurationMs": 4161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For pure cubic and quadratic functions, this\nequivalence point happens at 2/3 – that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102810,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when x^3 starts growing faster than x^2, but\nfor water droplets it’s somewhere around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107390,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a few million molecules; way too many to randomly\nclump together in less than the age of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112090,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113090,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And thus, raindrops are impossible for the\nprecise mathematical fact that x squared grows"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117430,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "faster than x cubed – for small numbers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120010,
    "dDurationMs": 4679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, so obviously raindrops exist, but if you\nwant to know HOW they sidestep this battle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124689,
    "dDurationMs": 3401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between quadratics and cubics, you’ll have\nto go watch MinuteEarth’s video about how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128090,
    "dDurationMs": 44420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "raindrops form."
    } ]
  } ]
}
